import{_ as a,o as s,c as t,a3 as r}from"./chunks/framework.DH50UWNB.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"Upgrade","description":"Upgrade the Wake server and the wake CLI: which command to run, what upgrades by itself, specific versions, upgrade backups and new-release notices.","frontmatter":{"description":"Upgrade the Wake server and the wake CLI: which command to run, what upgrades by itself, specific versions, upgrade backups and new-release notices."},"headers":[],"relativePath":"run/upgrade.md","filePath":"run/upgrade.md","lastUpdated":1794486787000}'),n={name:"run/upgrade.md"};function o(i,e,d,h,l,c){return s(),t("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[r('<h1 id="upgrade" tabindex="-1">Upgrade <a class="header-anchor" href="#upgrade" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Upgrade&quot;">​</a></h1><p>Wake has two parts, and both upgrade: the <strong>server</strong>, with the API and the web app, on the machine that runs Wake, and the <strong><code>wake</code> CLI</strong>, on every machine where you or your agents work. Upgrade the server first; the CLIs follow it.</p><h2 id="which-command-to-run" tabindex="-1">Which command to run <a class="header-anchor" href="#which-command-to-run" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Which command to run&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The command depends on the machine you are on:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Where you are</th><th>Run</th><th>What it upgrades</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>On the server&#39;s machine</td><td><code>wake upgrade</code></td><td>The server and the web app first, then the CLI to the server&#39;s new version</td></tr><tr><td>On the server&#39;s machine, server only</td><td><code>wake-server upgrade</code></td><td>The server and the web app</td></tr><tr><td>On any other machine</td><td><code>wake upgrade</code></td><td>The CLI, to the version your server recommends</td></tr><tr><td>Anywhere, to look without changing anything</td><td><code>wake upgrade --check</code></td><td>Nothing: it prints whether a newer CLI exists</td></tr></tbody></table><p><code>wake-server upgrade</code> moves to the newest stable release, skipping prereleases. You rarely need to run these commands by hand, because both parts also upgrade by themselves.</p><h2 id="what-upgrades-by-itself" tabindex="-1">What upgrades by itself <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-upgrades-by-itself" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What upgrades by itself&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The server upgrades once a day, and each CLI upgrades at its next agent session once its server has moved. So the server goes first and the CLIs follow by themselves.</p><h3 id="upgrade-on-a-schedule" tabindex="-1">Upgrade on a schedule <a class="header-anchor" href="#upgrade-on-a-schedule" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Upgrade on a schedule&quot;">​</a></h3><p>A new installation upgrades its server every day at 04:17, in the machine&#39;s local time, unless it was installed with <code>--no-auto-upgrade</code>. Turn it on or off, or see which it is, with:</p><div class="language-sh vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">sh</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line prompt"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">wake-server</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> auto-upgrade</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> on</span></span>\n<span class="line prompt"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">wake-server</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> auto-upgrade</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> off</span></span>\n<span class="line prompt"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">wake-server</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> auto-upgrade</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> status</span></span></code></pre></div><p><code>on</code> adds one line to your crontab that runs <code>wake-server upgrade</code>, with the <code>PATH</code> you ran it with (cron&#39;s own often lacks <code>docker</code>). It needs <code>crontab</code>. Each run is an ordinary server upgrade: backup first, rollback when the new version is not healthy, nothing changed when already current.</p><p>Each run logs to <code>auto-upgrade.log</code> in <code>&lt;your installation directory&gt;</code>. When the log grows past 1 MiB, it moves to <code>auto-upgrade.log.1</code>, replacing the previous one, so it never takes more than about 2 MiB.</p><p>The daily upgrade never touches the CLI. Turn it off when an unattended restart of the server would disturb your team, and upgrade by hand instead.</p><h3 id="the-cli-at-its-next-session" tabindex="-1">The CLI at its next session <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-cli-at-its-next-session" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The CLI at its next session&quot;">​</a></h3><p>When an agent session starts in a connected repository and the server recommends a newer CLI, the CLI upgrades itself in the background, and the session says <code>the wake CLI 2.4.0 is upgrading to 2.4.1 in the background</code>. It does this only when:</p><ul><li>the CLI was installed globally, with <code>npm install --global</code> or the package manager you installed it with (not when it runs through <code>npx</code>);</li><li>the <code>CI</code> environment variable is empty;</li><li><code>WAKE_AUTO_UPGRADE</code> is not <code>off</code>.</li></ul><p>It tries each version at most once a day, so a failing install does not run at every session. Its output goes to <code>~/.config/wake/auto-upgrade.log</code> (under <code>XDG_CONFIG_HOME</code> when you set it). It never upgrades a server.</p><p>Claude Code&#39;s <code>wake</code> plugin updates itself from Wake&#39;s marketplace; there is nothing to run.</p><h2 id="why-the-server-goes-first" tabindex="-1">Why the server goes first <a class="header-anchor" href="#why-the-server-goes-first" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Why the server goes first&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A newer CLI may send fields an older server refuses, while a newer server accepts every older CLI. That is why <code>wake upgrade</code> on the server&#39;s machine upgrades the server before the CLI, and why the other CLIs wait for the server to recommend their new version.</p><p>When the CLI is newer than its server, <code>wake upgrade</code> installs nothing and prints <code>wake 2.5.0 is newer than this server (2.4.0); `wake upgrade --version 2.4.0` installs the server&#39;s version</code>. Upgrade the server, or install the server&#39;s version as it says.</p><h2 id="what-a-server-upgrade-does" tabindex="-1">What a server upgrade does <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-a-server-upgrade-does" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What a server upgrade does&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>wake-server upgrade</code> upgrades the server and the web app together, since the web app ships with the server. It runs these steps:</p><ol><li>Downloads the new release&#39;s files and their signed checksums, verifies the signature with the release key your installation already has (a new release cannot bring its own key), then each file against the checksums. A missing release, a missing or wrong signature, or a mismatch stops it with nothing changed and no backup.</li><li>Stops the server and the web app, so nothing writes after the backup, and writes a backup to <code>backups/</code> (see <a href="/wake-dist/docs/run/backup">Back up and restore</a>). When the backup fails, it starts them again and stops with nothing changed: the old version keeps running.</li><li>Replaces the installation&#39;s files, <code>LICENSE</code> included, and sets the new version in <code>.env</code>, keeping your settings and secrets. It pulls the new images and starts them; the server applies new database migrations as it starts.</li><li>Waits up to two minutes for every service to be healthy and, on an installation without <code>--domain</code>, up to two more for <code>&lt;your Wake URL&gt;/health</code> to answer. Then it prints <code>Upgraded Wake 2.3.0 → 2.4.0. Backup: &lt;file&gt;</code>.</li><li>Removes the older upgrade backups, keeping the one it just wrote (see <a href="#upgrade-backups-kept">Upgrade backups kept</a>), and prints how many it removed and the space they used, such as <code>Removed 1 older upgrade backup, 589 MiB.</code></li></ol><p>When the new version is not healthy in time, the upgrade rolls back: it puts back the previous files and version, restores the database from the upgrade&#39;s backup (the new server may have migrated it), starts the previous version, and exits <code>1</code> saying it rolled back. Evidence files stay as they are. When the restore or the start fails, it exits <code>1</code> naming <code>wake-server restore &lt;backup&gt;</code>, which finishes the job.</p><p>An installation that already runs the newest release prints that it is up to date and changes nothing, except that it writes the installed release&#39;s <code>LICENSE</code> when the installation has none.</p><p>Downgrades are refused: a version lower than the installed one exits <code>1</code>, because migrations only move forward. To go back, restore a backup. One <code>upgrade</code>, <code>backup</code> or <code>restore</code> runs at a time per installation; a second one exits <code>1</code>, and a lock left by a killed command does not block the next one.</p><h2 id="what-a-cli-upgrade-does" tabindex="-1">What a CLI upgrade does <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-a-cli-upgrade-does" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What a CLI upgrade does&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>wake upgrade</code> installs the version your server recommends, which is always the server&#39;s own version. Outside a connected repository, it installs the newest release instead. Before it installs anything, it checks the release&#39;s signed checksums with the Wake release key; a release that is not signed or does not verify installs nothing.</p><p>It installs with the package manager that installed the CLI, such as <code>npm install --global</code> for a global npm install. When the CLI runs through <code>npx</code>, which installs nothing, it prints the <code>npx</code> command to run instead. See also <a href="/wake-dist/docs/start/cli#update-the-cli">Update the CLI</a>.</p><h2 id="upgrade-to-a-specific-version" tabindex="-1">Upgrade to a specific version <a class="header-anchor" href="#upgrade-to-a-specific-version" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Upgrade to a specific version&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Name the version to install one release instead of the newest. Prereleases are included:</p><div class="language-sh vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">sh</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line prompt"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">wake-server</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> upgrade</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --version</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> v2.4.0</span></span>\n<span class="line prompt"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">wake</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> upgrade</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --version</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> 2.4.0</span></span></code></pre></div><p><code>wake-server upgrade --version</code> refuses a version lower than the installed one. <code>wake upgrade --version</code> installs that CLI version even when it is older, and never touches the server; <code>wake upgrade --check</code> does not either.</p><h2 id="upgrade-backups-kept" tabindex="-1">Upgrade backups kept <a class="header-anchor" href="#upgrade-backups-kept" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Upgrade backups kept&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Each upgrade&#39;s backup is a full copy of the database and the evidence files, so a healthy upgrade keeps only the newest upgrade backup, the one it just wrote, and removes the older ones.</p><p>To keep more, set <code>WAKE_UPGRADE_BACKUPS</code> in the installation&#39;s <code>.env</code> to a whole number from 1 to 100, for example:</p><div class="language-sh vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">sh</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line prompt"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">WAKE_UPGRADE_BACKUPS</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">3</span></span></code></pre></div><p>The backup just written counts as one, and the others are ordered by the time they record. A missing or empty value means 1. Any other value keeps every backup, and the upgrade still runs and prints:</p><div class="language-text vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">text</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span>! WAKE_UPGRADE_BACKUPS in .env must be a whole number from 1 to 100: every backup was kept</span></span></code></pre></div><p>An upgrade never removes backups written by <code>wake-server backup</code> or <code>wake-server restore</code>, nor any other file in <code>backups/</code>. An upgrade that fails, rolls back or finds nothing to upgrade removes nothing. A backup it cannot remove prints <code>! could not remove &lt;file&gt;</code>, and the upgrade still succeeds.</p><h2 id="how-you-hear-of-a-new-release" tabindex="-1">How you hear of a new release <a class="header-anchor" href="#how-you-hear-of-a-new-release" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;How you hear of a new release&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Wake tells you about a new release in three places.</p><p><strong>The web app.</strong> Every Wake server asks GitHub for the newest stable release when it starts and every 12 hours, with a 5-second timeout. It sends nothing but a <code>User-Agent: wake-server/&lt;version&gt;</code> header, and a failed check never delays start-up or a request. When a newer release exists, signed-in people see a banner, such as <code>Wake 2.4.1 is available. This server runs 2.4.0.</code>, with links to the release notes and the upgrade guide. <strong>Dismiss</strong> hides it until the next release.</p><p><strong><code>wake-server status</code>.</strong> It asks GitHub for the newest release itself and prints <code>Wake 2.4.1 is available: run &lt;your installation directory&gt;/wake-server upgrade</code>, whatever the server&#39;s setting.</p><p><strong>The CLI.</strong> After commands such as <code>wake login</code>, <code>wake sync</code> and <code>wake watch</code>, and when an agent session starts, the CLI prints one line when it is behind its server, such as <code>wake: wake 2.4.1 is available for this server (you have 2.4.0); run `wake upgrade`</code>. It reads its server&#39;s version at most once an hour. It also reads GitHub&#39;s releases, at most once a day and never in CI, and names the newest one when its server recommends no version.</p><p>To turn the server&#39;s check off, add this line to the installation&#39;s <code>.env</code>, then run <code>wake-server start</code>, which recreates the server with it; upgrades keep the line:</p><div class="language-sh vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">sh</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line prompt"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">WAKE_UPDATE_CHECK</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">off</span></span></code></pre></div><p>No request then goes to GitHub, and the banner never shows. A server you run from the images yourself takes <code>UPDATE_CHECK=off</code> in its environment instead; see <a href="/wake-dist/docs/reference/environment">Server environment</a>.</p><p>To turn the CLI&#39;s release check off, set <code>WAKE_NO_UPDATE_CHECK=1</code>. The line that compares the CLI with its server still shows.</p><h2 id="when-the-server-is-somewhere-else" tabindex="-1">When the server is somewhere else <a class="header-anchor" href="#when-the-server-is-somewhere-else" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;When the server is somewhere else&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>wake upgrade</code> looks for the server&#39;s <code>wake-server</code> in this order:</p><ol><li>The directory in <code>WAKE_SERVER_DIR</code>, when you set it. When that directory holds no executable <code>wake-server</code>, the command says so and exits <code>1</code>, after upgrading the CLI.</li><li>The directory the installer recorded, so an installation made with <code>--dir</code> is found.</li><li>The default directory.</li></ol><p>When none holds an installation, <code>wake upgrade</code> upgrades the CLI only. <code>wake upgrade --no-server</code> skips the server even when it finds one. When the server upgrade fails, the CLI is still upgraded and the command exits <code>1</code>.</p><p>A server on another machine is upgraded by whoever runs it, on that machine. A server you run from the images yourself follows <a href="/wake-dist/docs/run/images">Run the images yourself</a>.</p><h2 id="older-installations" tabindex="-1">Older installations <a class="header-anchor" href="#older-installations" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Older installations&quot;">​</a></h2><p>An installation older than v2.4 may need one step by hand before or after its next upgrade: see <a href="/wake-dist/docs/run/older-installations">Older installations</a>.</p>',58)])])}const g=a(n,[["render",o]]);export{u as __pageData,g as default};
